re reported.  91 programs that do not load at    all were counted as working, `blackjack` among them.  - It ran everything bare, so `cribbage` ("Environment variable TERM not    defined"), `crib` ("Unknown terminal type ''"), `aterm` and `snake` were    all recorded as failures.  All four work.Host environment variables do not reach an OS-9 program: setting TERM in theshell that launches os9exec changes nothing.  Going through `SYS/login` is theonly way to give a program its environment.--------------------------------------------------------------THE 52, GROUPED BY CAUSE--------------------------------------------------------------WANTS FLOATING-POINT HARDWARE -- 1.  It executes 68881 instructions with nocoprocessor present.  Microware's `fpu` module is the emulator for exactlythis, and it is not on this disk.  config      prints char, short, int, long, pointer and float properties              correctly, then aborts on vector $0B where `double' begins`os9lib' was the second entry here until 2026-08-19.  It is not a program atall -- it is the RTF Fortran RUN-TIME LIBRARY, and running a library as aprogram is what reached its floating-point code.  See DOC/README-FORTRAN.WANTS THE ATARI `Graph' TRAP LIBRARY -- 7.  `CMDS/GAMES/graph` IS thatlibrary; it is a type-$0B trap module, not a game.  RENAMED 2026-08-19.  The module's own name was lowercase `graph' while all  seven programs ask for `Graph'.  os9exec only ever found it through a  case-insensitive HOST filename lookup; real OS-9 matches module names  exactly, so on real hardware none of these could have worked.  One byte in  the M$Name string, then the CRC recomputed; header parity is unaffected  because the name sits past the 48-byte header.  CRC and parity were checked  good BEFORE the patch as well as after.  All seven now START.  With OS9MDIR pointed at CMDS/GAMES both F$TLink calls  succeed -- `Math' and `Graph' -- and the library then takes a BUS ERROR  (vector $08, E_BUSERR 108) on its own first instruction.  AND IT CANNOT DO OTHERWISE HERE, established 2026-08-20.  os9exec does not  run guest code in supervisor state at all: the module attribute word is  read and handed back to callers (F$Link and friends) but never tested for  the supervisor bit, and the emulator's own source says the supervisor bit  is "kept as an honest guard in case emulated supervisor code is ever run".  So a type-$0B library with M$Attr $A0 -- bit 5, supervisor -- faults the  moment a user-state process enters it.  That is a missing emulator  FEATURE, not a defect to file, and it applies equally to `rxmod' and  `trap', whose VMod_trap library carries the same bit.  WHY, answered 2026-08-19 from the library's own documentation, recovered  from EFFO forum disk 4 and now at DOC/graph/graph.doc:    "...laeuft auf einem Atari-ST mit monochromem Monitor... ist das Modul     noch eigenstaendig und nicht in normale Programme einlinkbar, da es mit     gesetztem Supervisor-Bit laeuft."  It runs with the SUPERVISOR BIT SET and is explicitly not linkable into  ordinary programs; the author says a trap handler was planned for a later  version that never arrived.  The module agrees: M$Attr is $A0, and bit 5  of that is the supervisor-state bit.  A user-state process calling into it  faults immediately, which is exactly what happens here.  So these seven need an Atari ST with a monochrome monitor AND a system that  will run the library in supervisor state.  Source (graph.a and the demos)  is now at SRC/graph, and two more manuals at DOC/graph.  A. Greulich, 1988,  public domain.  g  striche  apfel  sine  showpic  graphdemo  graphsaveWANTS A TRAP LIBRARY -- 2.  One of them is now supplied.  rxmod       asks F$TLink for `VMod_trap'.  That library was found in the              pool (EFFO forum 15, the SERLOAD utilities) and is now at              CMDS/COMMS/vmod_trap, with its assembler source in SRC/serload.              Its module name